<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cusco</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cuzco, Qosqo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cusco is a city in the Andes of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, about 3,400 metres above sea level, which was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>. Its palaces and temples, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Coricancha</a>, were rebuilt by the Spaniards after 1533 on Inca foundations, and the fortress-shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán</a> stands above it. The city is a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cusco.jpg" alt="Cusco" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: tomscoffin · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_Calle_Suecia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Incas began as one of many small ethnic groups in the southern Peruvian highlands, centred on the <b>Cusco</b> Valley, where a local kingdom took shape from around the thirteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>These skills outlived the kingdom: after the conquest the Incas carried Chimú metalworkers to <b>Cusco</b> to make gold and silver ornaments for their temples and palaces, and sent others to the region of Lake Titicaca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-chimor">Kingdom of Chimor · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire, Francisco Pizarro executed Atahualpa in 1533, entered <b>Cusco</b> and installed Manco Inca, a son of Huayna Capac, as a puppet ruler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As heir to Inca <b>Cuzco</b> and colonial Lima, the Peruvian republic carries one of the deepest cultural legacies in the Americas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Viceroyalty of Peru grew out of the conquest of the Inca Empire by Francisco Pizarro and his companions, who captured the Sapa Inca Atahualpa at Cajamarca in 1532 and entered <b>Cuzco</b> in 1533.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Cerro Baúl stands on a flat-topped mountain with steep sides above the Moquegua valley, and Pikillacta near <b>Cusco</b>, enclosed by high walls, lay in a basin whose entrances appear to have been fortified.</blockquote><a href="/empire/wari-empire">Wari Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
